---
title: Legal pages
description: Publishing your terms of service and privacy policy.
section: Compliance
---

**Compliance → Legal Pages** is where you write the two documents every product needs: a **terms of
service** and a **privacy policy**. Both are authored in Markdown, in the dashboard, with a live
preview.

## Where they end up

Published documents are served publicly, per application:

```text
/terms/{clientId}
/privacy/{clientId}
```

There are also platform-wide fallbacks at `/terms` and `/privacy`, used when a client has no
document of its own. The hosted login and sign-up pages link to whichever applies, so a user can
always read what they are agreeing to before they agree to it.

## Versioning and acceptance

Publishing a new version does not silently re-consent your existing users.

Acceptance is tracked per version, against the `POLICY_TERMS_VERSION` and `POLICY_PRIVACY_VERSION`
values configured on the server. Bumping one of those tells GateKeeper that what users previously
accepted is no longer current, and it can require them to accept again. Who has accepted what is
visible on each user's [Privacy tab](/docs/data-subject-requests), which is what makes the
acceptance auditable rather than merely asserted.

> [!NOTE]
> Editing the document text without bumping the version changes what new users see, but does not
> ask existing users to re-accept. That is the right behaviour for fixing a typo and the wrong
> behaviour for a substantive change — the distinction is yours to make.

## The Markdown is sanitized

Content is admin-authored Markdown, rendered to HTML and sanitized before it reaches a browser.

That sanitization is not paranoia about you; it is defence in depth. These pages are linked
directly from the login screen, so a script injected into a privacy policy would execute on a page
adjacent to where users type their password. Scripts and event handlers are stripped, and there is
no way to turn that off.

> [!TIP]
> Write the actual documents. A terms page that says "TODO" is worse than none — it is a public
> statement that you have not thought about this, on a page your users are being asked to agree to.
